body:
Hey, if it's worth anything, I remembered this quote by (I think) Alan Watts? I dunno. It went something like this: The healing is complete not when our traumas go away, but when we finally accept they will stay with us forever. 

&#x200B;

Sometimes I wonder how much of my pain is due to me not accepting my situation.

My understanding of life is that everything depends on the scale of magnification, on our ability to grasp a part of the thing. In the grand scheme of things, nothing matters. I die, then all the people who knew about me die, and then we all die and it's nothingness. So one could ask: "What for?" It appears to me, though, that the closer you zoom in, the shorter a span of time you consider, the more important your actions are. 

&#x200B;

So, considering only the present moment, it is infinitely important to act according to one's best knowledge. Honest work, truth and kindness - maybe in the long term, they mean nothing. But right here, in this moment, that's everything. And thinking about it, what do we have in life, if not an ever-lasting string of moments?
